From 1c805f440a53122de93d6d1592cf3b7040fab48e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GTA2 port <port@localhost>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 19:35:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] native: script commands keep 4-byte pointer slots (handles on
 LP64)

The .scr script is loaded as bytes and its commands are read in place
through the SCR_* structures, ten of which hold a run-time pointer in a
4-byte slot (SCR_POINTER, SCR_PLAYER_PED, SCR_CHAR_DATA_DEC, SCR_OBJ_DATA,
SCR_THREAD, SCR_CHAR_AREA_ANY, SCR_CRANE_TARGET_DEC, SCR_CRANE_BASIC_DEC,
SCR_DOOR_DATA_DEC, SCR_ARROW_ENTITY). With 8-byte pointers every later
field would move (SCR_PLAYER_PED's position would be read from 0x10
instead of 0xC) and each store would overwrite the next command's bytes;
no size assert covered them.

These slots are now GTA2_SCR_PTR(T) (Source/port_scr_ptr.hpp): a plain T*
outside the native LP64 build, a 4-byte handle into a pointer table there
(port/native/platform/scr_handles.cpp), handle 0 being nullptr. Native
offsetof asserts check each command's fields against the offsets in their
names. One C-style cast that went straight from the slot to another
pointer type converts to the slot's own type first.
